.TH ZDIVTEST 1
.SH NAME
zdivtest \- test that integer division by zero raises an exception
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zdivtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Zdivtest
checks that integer division and modulo by zero raise the Dis
.B "zero divide"
exception, for
.BR int ,
.B big
and
.BR byte ,
and that an enclosing
.B "{} exception {}"
block catches it and execution resumes afterwards.
It also checks that ordinary division by a non-zero divisor is
undisturbed, including the sign of a negative quotient and remainder;
without those a guard that always raised would pass every other case.
.PP
Each case prints
.B ok
or
.BR FAIL ,
and the last line is a count.
.PP
.B "Run it under both"
.B -c0
.B "and"
.BR -c1 .
The two execution paths raise from different code \-
.B libinterp/xec.c
interpreted,
.B libinterp/comp-arm64.c
compiled \- and a run under one says nothing at all about the other.
.IP
.EX
emu -c0 /dis/sh.dis -c zdivtest
emu -c1 /dis/sh.dis -c zdivtest
.EE
.PP
On AArch64 the
.B SDIV
instruction yields 0 for a zero divisor and does not trap, so neither
path gets the exception for free the way x86 does from
.BR SIGFPE :
each has to test the divisor itself. Before this test existed the
interpreter was fixed and the compiler was not, and the fix was recorded
as complete \- which is why running both settings is part of the test
rather than a suggestion.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/zdivtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR emu (1)
.SH BUGS
It does not cover
.BR real
division: IEEE arithmetic defines division by zero as an infinity, and
.B divf
is deliberately left without a check.
.PP
It does not cover the other JIT back ends. On x86 the hardware traps and
the exception arrives through
.BR SIGFPE ,
so the defect this tests for cannot occur there; the remaining
back ends
.RB ( comp-arm.c ,
.BR comp-mips.c ,
.BR comp-power.c ,
.BR comp-sparc.c ,
.BR comp-spim.c )
were not examined and are untested.
.PP
Each division sits in the same stack frame as its handler. This build
crashes outright when an exception unwinds through a frame that has no
handler of its own, so a deeper raise would test that defect instead of
this one.
.PP
It does not cover division by a zero that arrives in the
.B middle
operand rather than the source operand, nor the
.B IDIVX
family used for array indexing.
